<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ADR 0008 — cleartext to the user's server, and only there.

  The base configuration has to permit cleartext because we cannot know the hosts
  in advance: a provider is a hostname somebody types into a form, most panels
  serve their streams, logos and guide over plain HTTP on a high port, and there
  is no list to allow. Refusing it means refusing the product's central promise
  on its main platform — and it is the very promise ADR 0007 leans on when it
  tells a browser user to open the applications instead.

  The domain rule is what keeps that from being a blanket. `api.lumo.tv` carries
  the session tokens and every piece of account data, and it is the one host whose
  address we know at build time; downgrading it has to be impossible rather than
  merely unlikely, so it is refused by policy and not by everybody remembering to
  type https.

  Never `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"` instead of this file: that is the
  same permission with no exception for our own API, which is exactly the part
  that must not be downgradable.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">api.lumo.tv</domain>
    </domain-config>

</network-security-config>
